analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment at end of set method.

Comment: "// @@@ If the index is greater than or equal to 0 and less than the initial size of the vector, then the result is equal to the old value at that index."

We have method:

public synchronized E set(int index, E element) {
    if (index >= elementCount)
        throw new ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException(index);

    E oldValue = elementData(index);
    elementData[index] = element;
    return oldValue;
    // @@@ If the index is greater than or equal to 0 and less than the initial size of the vector, then the result is equal to the old value at that index.
}

We need to refer to initial size (before modification). That's elementCount before change. Since set does not change size, elementCount remains same, but we need old size. Use \old(this.size()) or \old(this.elementCount) but elementCount is protected field; we should use public observer method size(). So we use \old(this.size()).

Premise: index >=0 && index < \old(this.size()) => \result.equals(\old(this.elementAt(index)))? Wait we need old value at that index. There is method elementAt(int) which returns component. That's an observer. So we can use this.elementAt(index) after modification? But after set, elementAt(index) returns new element, not old. So we need old value: \old(this.elementAt(index)). Use that.

Equality: Since E is generic, we may use .equals. In examples they used .equals for objects. So assert: index >=0 && index < \old(this.size()) => \result.equals(\old(this.elementAt(index)));

But need parentheses: (index >=0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.elementAt(index)));

Make sure to reference index variable, which is parameter.

Thus final answer:

<code>assert (index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size())) => \result.equals(\old(this.elementAt(index)));</code>

Check syntax: Use parentheses around premise. Should be fine.

